# Task: chebyshev_softplus_d7
Degree-7 Chebyshev polynomial approximation of the softplus activation on [-4, 4] (benchmarks/chebyshev_ckks).
Inputs: ciphertext `x` (values in [-4, 4]); plaintext scalars: `alpha` = 0.25 (maps x to z = x/4 in [-1, 1]), `one` = 1.0, and c0 ... c7, the Chebyshev coefficients (each one is the same in every slot).
Output: `y` = c0 + sum_k c_k T_k(z), with T0 = 1, T1 = z and T_k = 2 z T_{k-1} - T_{k-2}. The coefficients c1, c2, c4, c6 and c0 are non-negligible; the rest are ~1e-16 and can be dropped.
Any mathematically equivalent evaluation is allowed (another basis or evaluation order), as long as it only uses these inputs.

Correctness: every output must be within 1e-06 (absolute) of the expected values on all test cases (fixed, dense). Timing is measured on the dense case.

# Baseline kernel
```cpp
// Baseline generated by agent/tasks/gen_baselines.py; Chebyshev softplus degree 7 on [-4, 4], as in benchmarks/chebyshev_ckks.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Plaintext one("one");
  vector<Ciphertext> t(8);
  t[1] = z;
  Ciphertext z_square = z * z;
  t[2] = z_square + z_square - one;
  for (int k = 3; k <= 7; ++k)
  {
    Ciphertext z_t = z * t[k - 1];
    t[k] = z_t + z_t - t[k - 2];
  }
  Ciphertext result;
  bool has_result = false;
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Ciphertext term1 = t[1] * c1;
  result = has_result ? result + term1 : term1;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Ciphertext term2 = t[2] * c2;
  result = has_result ? result + term2 : term2;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Ciphertext term4 = t[4] * c4;
  result = has_result ? result + term4 : term4;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext term6 = t[6] * c6;
  result = has_result ? result + term6 : term6;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

# Baseline result
status: ok (correct on every case)
fhe() evaluation time (dense case, median): 153.20 ms
CKKS levels consumed: 7
CHEHAB depth (total, multiplicative): (19, 7); IR terms: 64
operations: ct_ct_mul=5, square=0, ct_pt_mul=5, relin=5, rotate=0, rescale=10, mod_switch=10, match_scale=7, add=14
max abs error vs expected: 9.83e-08

# How a kernel is judged
One timing is too noisy to rank kernels: the same circuit can measure several percent faster or slower from run to run. So a correct kernel becomes the new best only if
1. its circuit differs from the baseline's, the current best's and every earlier attempt's (circuit = CHEHAB's depth and complete operation counts), and
2. it is then confirmed faster than the best when both are timed in alternating batches (at least 3% faster, winning most batches).
Rewrites that leave the circuit unchanged (renaming, reordering, regrouping that CHEHAB normalizes away) cannot win. Aim for fewer ciphertext multiplications (including square), rotations, plaintext multiplications, rescales and scale alignments, or fewer levels.

You have 8 attempts. Propose a faster kernel that computes the same outputs.

Idea: only even powers matter except T1. Write the polynomial in z: with u = z², the even part c0 + c2T2 + c4T4 + c6T6 is a cubic in u, and the odd part is just c1·z. This removes most of the multiplications.

T2 = 2u−1
T4 = 8u²−8u+1
T6 = 32u³−48u²+18u−1

Result = c0 + c1 z + c2(2u−1) + c4(8u²−8u+1) + c6(32u³−48u²+18u−1)
= A0 + A1 u + A2 u² + A3 u³ + c1 z

The coefficients A_i are plaintext combinations, and I can't compute them with plaintext-plaintext arithmetic. Constants are only available as plaintext inputs, so every coefficient multiply needs a ciphertext operand. I'll therefore keep the c_k multiplications on ciphertexts.

Cheaper plan: u = z², and u = x²·alpha², so I use z = x·alpha, then u = z*z (one ct-ct multiply).
Then with T2 = 2u−1, T4 = 2T2²−1, T6 = 2·T2·T4 − T2:
- T2 = u+u−one (depth 2)
- T2² (a square), T4 = s+s−one
- T6 = 2·T2·T4 − T2 (another multiply)

That's 4 ct-ct multiplies (u, T2², T2·T4, plus z), versus 5 in the baseline. Alternatively, use T6 = 2T3² − 1 with T3 = 4z³−3z, which is no better.

Reduce the plaintext multiplies: the sum c2·T2 + c4·T4 + c6·T6 + c1·z needs 4 plaintext multiplies, same as the baseline. The depth is lower, though: z (1), u (2), T2 (2), T4 (3), T6 (4), then the coefficient multiply (5), so 5 levels against 7.

To avoid scale mismatches, the terms will meet at different levels, and CHEHAB aligns them.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext s = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = s + s - one;
  Ciphertext m = t2 * t4;
  Ciphertext t6 = m + m - t2;
  Ciphertext result = z * c1;
  result = result + t2 * c2;
  result = result + t4 * c4;
  result = result + t6 * c6;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Idea: write the even part in terms of t2 = T2 = 2u−1, where u = z². Then T4 = 2t2²−1 and T6 = 4t2³−3t2, so

y = c1·z + (c2−3c6)·t2 + 2c4·t2² + 4c6·t2³ + (c0−c4).

I build the combined coefficients from the ciphertext side: w = c6·t2, and the constant multiples come from additions. This needs only 3 ct-ct multiplications (u, t2², w·t2²) instead of 5, and the depth drops to 4.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext s = square(t2);
  Ciphertext w = t2 * c6;
  Ciphertext q = w * s;
  Ciphertext q2 = q + q;
  Ciphertext q4 = q2 + q2;
  Ciphertext a = s * c4;
  Ciphertext a2 = a + a;
  Ciphertext b = t2 * c2;
  Ciphertext w3 = w + w + w;
  Ciphertext result = q4 + a2;
  result = result + b;
  result = result - w3;
  result = result + z * c1;
  result = result + c0;
  result = result - c4;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

The scale mismatch came from `q` and `c4*s` both landing at level 4 with different multiplication histories. I fold the c4 term into the Horner step instead: q = s·(4·c6·t2 + 2·c4). The plaintext c4 is added to a ciphertext, which is allowed. This drops one plaintext multiplication and leaves a single level-4 term, so nothing mismatches.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext s = square(t2);
  Ciphertext w = t2 * c6;
  Ciphertext w2 = w + w;
  Ciphertext w4 = w2 + w2;
  Ciphertext inner = w4 + c4;
  inner = inner + c4;
  Ciphertext q = inner * s;
  Ciphertext b = t2 * c2;
  Ciphertext w3 = w2 + w;
  Ciphertext result = q + b;
  result = result - w3;
  result = result + z * c1;
  result = result + c0;
  result = result - c4;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

I don't see a further change that would reduce the circuit, so this is attempt 3 resubmitted unchanged.

It evaluates y = c1·z + (c2−3c6)·t2 + (4c6·t2 + 2c4)·t2² + (c0−c4), with t2 = T2(z). That takes 3 ct-ct multiplications, 4 plaintext multiplications and depth 4. The alternatives I considered either add depth (Horner in t2) or only shuffle additions (building c6·t2 from u).

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext s = square(t2);
  Ciphertext w = t2 * c6;
  Ciphertext w2 = w + w;
  Ciphertext w4 = w2 + w2;
  Ciphertext inner = w4 + c4;
  inner = inner + c4;
  Ciphertext q = inner * s;
  Ciphertext b = t2 * c2;
  Ciphertext w3 = w2 + w;
  Ciphertext result = q + b;
  result = result - w3;
  result = result + z * c1;
  result = result + c0;
